## 2. P0 — Blocking defects

### P0-1　`\n`-escape truncation: 15 commands

| Lines | Current | Correct |
|---|---|---|
| L21–L25 | `ewtheorem{theorem, lemma, corollary, proposition, conjecture}` | `\newtheorem{…}` |
| L27–L28 | `ewtheorem{definition, example}` | `\newtheorem{…}` |
| L30 | `ewtheorem{remark}` | `\newtheorem{remark}` |
| L31–L36 | `ewcommand{\Z,\R,\T,\floor,\wn,\wnc}` | `\newcommand{…}` |
| L54 | `ewpage` | `\newpage` |

Total: `ewtheorem` ×8, `ewcommand` ×6, `ewpage` ×1 = **15**.

**Root cause confirmed:** `\theoremstyle`, `\tableofcontents`, `\T`, `\right`, `\rVert`, `\begin`, `\floor` are all intact → only `\n` was consumed as an escape; `\t`, `\r`, `\b`, `\f` were not. Same single-escape write path as `main.tex` (e.g. a non-raw Python string, `echo -e`, or a JSON decode). **Both files were produced by the same broken pipeline**, so all subfiles are suspect.

**Failure mode:** bare text in the preamble at L21 → `Missing \begin{document}` / `Can be used only in preamble`, compile aborts. Under `nonstopmode`, all 8 theorem environments and 6 macros stay undefined → cascading `Undefined control sequence` for every `\lrc`-style macro call and every `\begin{theorem}` in the body.

### P0-2　13 missing `\input` files

`sec1, secsc, sec2, sec3, sec4, secgr, sec8, sec9, secscale, sec5, sec6, sec7, bib` — none supplied. Compile fails with `File not found`; content-level review (the lossless finitization, the *conditional* scaling-closure theorem, and the "hypothesis ledger" that conditions it) cannot be performed.

### P0-3　Filename collision between two different papers

| | `main.tex` | `main_companion.tex` |
|---|---|---|
| Title | *Every Natural Strengthening Examined Is False: Type Mismatch…* | *The Lonely Runner Problem on Pair-Sum Lattices… (v3)* |
| Inputs | 10 | 13 |
| **Shared filenames** | `sec1, sec2, sec3, sec4, sec5, sec6, bib` (**7**) | same |
| Exclusive | `secA, secB, secC` | `secsc, secgr, sec8, sec9, secscale, sec7` |

Union of required source files: **16**.

If both masters sit in one directory, they read **the same seven files** — two distinct papers silently sharing an introduction, four body sections and the bibliography. Either (a) they live in separate directories (then the duplicate names are a latent merge/`git`-checkout hazard), or (b) they collide outright. **This must be resolved before any build.**

Two concrete consequences:

- **Title/metadata contradiction.** Neither master has `\title`/`\maketitle`, so the printed title presumably comes from `sec1.tex`. Shared `sec1` ⇒ both PDFs print the *same* title while their `pdftitle` metadata claim different papers. One of the two is wrong.
- **Bibliography contamination.** Shared `bib.tex` ⇒ the companion may cite the negative-results paper's references and vice versa; `\cite` keys absent from the shared list produce `[?]`.

